apply-patch — Apply a git patch file with verification and optional cleanup

USAGE:
    apply-patch <patch-file> [--cleanup] [--dry-run] [--stat] [--check-only] [--verbose] [--help]

ARGUMENTS:
    <patch-file>    Path to unified-diff patch file (required)

OPTIONS:
    --cleanup       Delete <patch-file> after successful apply (default: keep)
    --dry-run       Run git apply --check only; do not modify working tree
    --stat          Run git apply --stat and print summary; implies --dry-run
    --check-only    Alias for --dry-run
    --verbose       Print each git command before execution
    --help          Show this help and exit

EXIT CODES:
    0   Success (applied, or dry-run/check passed)
    1   Usage error / missing dependency / not in git repo
    2   Patch file not found or unreadable
    3   git apply --check failed (patch does not apply cleanly)
    4   git apply failed during actual application
    5   Cleanup failed (patch applied but rm failed)

EXAMPLES:
    # Preview what a patch would do
    apply-patch my-changes.patch --stat

    # Apply patch and keep the patch file
    apply-patch my-changes.patch

    # Apply patch and delete it afterward
    apply-patch my-changes.patch --cleanup

    # Dry-run check only (exit 3 if patch doesn't apply cleanly)
    apply-patch my-changes.patch --check-only

REQUIREMENTS:
    - git (>= 2.20)
    - bash (>= 4.0)
    - Must be run inside a git repository

SKILL:
    Part of git-apply-patch-cleanup (base skill)
    See SKILL.md for full documentation